e function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=-3{x}^{3},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(-3{x}^{3})}^{′} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =-9{x}^{2} \\\\  & \\ne -6{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=-3{x}^{3}$ and ${y}^{′}=-9{x}^{2}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{3y}{x} \\\\ -9{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{3(-3{x}^{3})}{x} \\\\ -9{x}^{2} & =-9{x}^{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=-3{x}^{3}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation"}]}
